    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Lake Mead National Recreation Area is located at the southern terminus of the regional carbonate-rock aquifer system in Nevada. Springs occurring to the North of Lake Mead represent a minor discharge point of the aquifer’s White River basin flow regime. The springs that discharge significant water from the carbonate-rock system form a spatial cluster occurring at or near the Rogers Spring fault. This fault is one of the numerous left-lateral strike-slip faults that comprise the Lake MeadFault system. The location of the springs relative to the strike-slip fault is related to increased permeability due to fault-normal opening along the faults. A two-dimensional boundary element model of echelon strike-slip faults shows significant fault-normal opening can occur in a step over region. The local variation in spring discharge along the Roger Spring fault relates to local extensional step over and fault intersections. These observations and model results support the concept that strike-slip faulting is instrumental in creating areas within or near the fault of increased permeability. The zones of increased permeability along the fault allow fluids at depth to move towards the surface where ground water is discharged and by extrapolation, such dilated zones along faults can provide pathways for any fluids such as magma, geothermal waters or hydrocarbons.",
